It is incredibly common for DevOps and Database engineers to be abstracted away from the physical layer when using Terraform. You define a vshere_virtual_disk or an AWS/Azure volume, and it just "appears."

However, when you are pushing production databases to their limit (like hitting 200+ MB/s and choking on checkpoints), you have to pierce that abstraction layer.

Here is your step-by-step roadmap to map your infrastructure from inside your Linux VM all the way down to the physical SAN controller, followed by how top-tier engineering organizations manage this.

Part 1: How to Trace from the VM to the SAN Controller
You can trace the entire path right from your command line and your infrastructure-as-code files.

Step 1: Map the Linux Device to the Virtual SCSI Controller
First, look at your VM's block devices to see how they are distributed. Run this inside your Linux VM:

Bash
lsblk -S
What you are looking for: You want to see the TRAN (transport) and driver type. If it says msdos or lpfc under a generic driver, or if all 20 devices share the exact same controller prefix (e.g., all are on lpfc controller 0), you have a virtual queue bottleneck.

Target: You want to see pvscsi listed as the driver for your database disks.

Step 2: Read Your Terraform Code to Check the Virtual Layout
Open the Terraform repository used to provision that specific database VM. Look for the vsphere_virtual_machine resource block and inspect how the disks are attached:

Terraform
# Example of what to look for in your TF code
resource "vsphere_virtual_machine" "pg_db" {
  # ...
  
  # Look at the scsi_controller_count
  scsi_controller_count = 4 

  disk {
    label        = "disk0"
    size         = 100
    unit_number  = 0 # Controller 0, Slot 0 (Usually OS)
  }

  disk {
    label        = "disk1"
    size         = 2000
    unit_number  = 15 # CRITICAL: 15 belongs to Controller 1!
  }
}
The Rule: In VMware, unit_number 0–14 belong to Controller 0. Numbers 15–29 belong to Controller 1. Numbers 30–44 belong to Controller 2.

If your Terraform files show 20 disk blocks and their unit_number values are just 0, 1, 2, 3 ... 14, then all your disks are crammed onto one controller. You need to modify your Terraform to distribute them across all 4 controllers.

Step 3: Find the Physical SAN Back-End (The "WWN")
To map your Linux disk to an actual physical LUN on a SAN controller, you need its World Wide Name (WWN) or Network Address Authority (NAA) ID. Run this inside Linux:

Bash
ls -l /dev/disk/by-id/
You will see output mapping your device names (sdb, sdc) to strings like this:

Plaintext
wwn-0x6000d310002b540000000000000012a4 -> ../../sdb
6000d310002b540000000000000012a4 is the exact, unique hardware identifier of the volume on the physical SAN storage array.

Copy this ID. Hand it to your Infrastructure/Storage team and say: "This is the disk experiencing datastore contention. What physical pool, controller ports, and RAID group does this NAA ID map to?"

Part 2: How "Giant Companies" Handle This Effort
At hyper-scale tech companies (Netflix, Uber, Apple) or enterprise financial institutions managing multi-petabyte database fleets, the absolute separation between "DevOps/DBA" and "Storage Admins" is eliminated through strict architecture patterns:

1. Dedicated Hardware (Bare Metal or Local NVMe)
For core tier-0 high-scale databases (like a 100TB Citus or large PostgreSQL cluster), giant companies completely avoid standard virtual SAN architectures.

Instead, they deploy databases on Bare Metal servers or specialized VM instances featuring Local NVMe SSDs physically attached directly to the motherboard (e.g., AWS i3en instances or on-prem equivalent servers).

Why? It bypasses the VMware scheduler, hypervisor queues, physical Fibre Channel switches, and SAN controller caches entirely. I/O latency drops from 2–5 milliseconds down to microseconds.

2. Infrastructure-as-Code Injects Custom Tags
If they do use a shared SAN fabric, the storage team builds pre-optimized storage profiles inside VMware. The DevOps team doesn't just provision a "disk"; they pass a highly specific storage policy variable in their Terraform code:

Terraform
storage_policy_id = data.vsphere_storage_policy.database_ultra_low_latency.id
Under the hood, that single tag automatically forces VMware and the SAN controller to provision the disk on isolated flash arrays, lock in a 10,000+ IOPS guarantee, and isolate the paths away from noisy neighbors.

3. Unified Observability (OpenTelemetry / Prometheus)
Giant companies do not look at iotop in a vacuum. They bridge the gap using end-to-end monitoring stacks:

The VM exports metrics via pg_stat_database and node-exporter to Prometheus.

VMware exports datastore latency metrics via vRealize or specialized exporters.

The SAN exports controller port saturation metrics.

All of these are overlaid on a single Grafana dashboard. When Postgres latency spikes, engineers can instantly see if the spike matches a SAN controller port utilization cliff, completely skipping the finger-pointing between teams.
